\f0\fs32 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs96 \cf2 Value semantics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo doesn't enforce value semantics or reference semantics. It supports them both and allows each type to define how it is created, copied, and moved (if at all). So, if you're building your own type, you can implement it to support value semantics, reference semantics, or a bit of both. That said, Mojo is designed with argument behaviors that default to value semantics, and it provides tight controls for reference semantics that avoid memory errors.\
The controls over reference semantics are provided by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 value ownership model}}, but before we get into the syntax and rules for that, it's important that you understand the principles of value semantics. Generally, it means that each variable has unique access to a value, and any code outside the scope of that variable cannot modify its value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Intro to value semantics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In the most basic situation, sharing a value-semantic type means that you create a copy of the value. This is also known as "pass by value." For example, consider this code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  x \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  y \cf5 =\cf13  x\cb1 \
\cb12     y \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf16 "x:"\cf5 ,\cf13  x\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf16 "y:"\cf5 ,\cf13  y\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 x: 1\cb1 \
\cb12 y: 2\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 We assigned the value of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which creates the value for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by making a copy of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . When we increment 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the value of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't change. Each variable has exclusive ownership of a value.\
Whereas, if a type instead uses reference semantics, then 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  would point to the same value as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and incrementing either one would affect the value for both. Neither 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  nor 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  would "own" the value, and any variable would be allowed to reference it and mutate it.\
Numeric values in Mojo are value semantic because they're trivial types, which are cheap to copy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Value semantics in Mojo functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Value semantics also apply to function arguments in Mojo by default. However, the way in which they apply differs depending on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}}, which is discussed in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Ownership}} page.\
For example, in the following function, the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument is immutable by default, so if the function wants to modify the value in the local scope, it needs to make a local copy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 add_two\cf5 (\cf13 y\cf5 :\cf13  Int\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf6 # y += 2  # This would cause a compiler error because `y` is immutable\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf6 # We can instead make an explicit copy:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  z \cf5 =\cf13  y\cb1 \
\cb12     z \cf5 +=\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf16 "z:"\cf5 ,\cf13  z\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb12 def\cf13  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 var\cf13  x \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     add_two\cf5 (\cf13 x\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb12     \cf5 print(\cf16 "x:"\cf5 ,\cf13  x\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb12 z: 3\cb1 \
\cb12 x: 1\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This is all consistent with value semantics because each variable maintains unique ownership of its value.\
The way the function receives the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value is a "look but don't touch" approach to value semantics. This is also a more memory-efficient approach when dealing with memory-intensive arguments, because Mojo doesn't make any copies unless we explicitly make the copies ourselves.\
Thus, the default behavior for function arguments is fully value semantic: arguments are immutable references, and any living variable from the caller is not affected by the function.\
But we must also allow reference semantics (mutable references) because it's how we build performant and memory-efficient programs (making copies of everything gets really expensive). The challenge is to introduce reference semantics in a way that does not disturb the predictability and safety of value semantics.\
The way we do that in Mojo is, instead of enforcing that every variable have "exclusive access" to a value, we ensure that every value has an "exclusive owner," and destroy each value when the lifetime of its owner ends.\
On the next page about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 value ownership}}, you'll learn how to modify the default argument conventions, and safely use reference semantics so every value has only one owner at a time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
